Actually, I want to reconsider the `readcfgfile` buffer handling. Let me re-read it very carefully:

```c
next_line:
        while (fgets(thisline + readbytes, linesize - readbytes, f) != NULL) {
                ...
                end = line + strlen(line);
                if (end-line == linesize-1 && *(end-1) != '\n') {
                        ...
                        readbytes = linesize - 1;
                        linesize = newlinesize;
                        thisline = newline;
                        continue;
                }
                readbytes = 0;
                ...
```

Wait, there's a subtle issue. When the line fills the buffer and reallocs, `readbytes = linesize - 1`. But the null terminator from the previous fgets is at `thisline[linesize-1]` (old linesize). After realloc, `thisline` points to the new buffer. The old content is copied. Then `fgets(thisline + readbytes, linesize - readbytes, f)` where `readbytes = old - 1` and `linesize = 2*old`. So `fgets(thisline + old - 1, old + 1, f)`. This reads at most `old` chars, null at `thisline[old-1 + old] = thisline[2*old - 1]`. In bounds.

But here's the thing: the null terminator from the first fgets is at `thisline[old-1]`. When the second fgets writes, it starts at `thisline[old-1]` and overwrites the null. So the line content is contiguous. Good.

